---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'AI-Era Defense Overview', href: '/docs/ai-defense' },
  { title: 'Allowlisting', href: '/docs/ai-defense/allowlisting' },
  { title: 'Attack-Surface Hardening', href: '/docs/ai-defense/hardening', active: true },
  { title: 'Deception as a 0-Day Sensor', href: '/docs/ai-defense/deception' },
  { title: 'Capture to Virtual Patch', href: '/docs/ai-defense/response' },
  { title: 'Case Study: Black October', href: '/docs/ai-defense/black-october' },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'idea', text: 'Unreachable Bugs Need No Patch', level: 2 },
  { id: 'plugins', text: 'Plugin Minimisation', level: 2 },
  { id: 'wordpress', text: 'WordPress Configuration', level: 2 },
  { id: 'containment', text: 'Post-Exploit Containment', level: 2 },
  { id: 'updates', text: 'Updates Without the Gamble', level: 2 },
  { id: 'checklist', text: 'Checklist', level: 2 },
  { id: 'honest', text: 'Honest Assessment', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Attack-Surface Hardening" description="Reduce how much vulnerable code is reachable, and limit what a successful exploit can do, without knowing which bug will be found next" section="AI-Era Defense" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    AI-assisted discovery multiplies the bugs found in code you <em>run</em>. It can't find a bug in code you
    removed, can't exploit one behind an endpoint that doesn't answer, and gains little from one it can't turn
    into persistence or data. Hardening is the layer that holds even when the
    <a href="/docs/ai-defense/allowlisting">allowlist</a> is bypassed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="idea">Unreachable Bugs Need No Patch</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard title="Remove" icon="archive" tag="Less code" description="Every plugin, theme and endpoint you delete takes all of its future CVEs with it." meta="smallest surface" />
    <DocCard title="Disable" icon="lock" tag="Less reach" description="Features you keep but don't expose (file editors, XML-RPC, user enumeration) stop being entry points." meta="config, not code" />
    <DocCard title="Contain" icon="box" tag="Less impact" description="Assume one exploit lands. Deny it outbound network, writable code paths and reusable credentials." meta="foothold ≠ control" />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="plugins">Plugin Minimisation</h2>
  <p>Most WordPress CVEs are in plugins, and AI-assisted auditing makes the long tail of small, rarely
  reviewed plugins the most productive target.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Audit with the dashboard.</strong> <a href="/docs/engine/wordpress-management">WordPress Studio</a>
    shows what the production shop runs. Every installed but inactive plugin is still reachable through its PHP
    files. Delete it, don't just deactivate it.</li>
    <li><strong>Prefer fewer, larger plugins.</strong> One well-maintained plugin with an active security
    process beats three single-purpose ones from volunteers.</li>
    <li><strong>Watch for abandoned plugins.</strong> No release in 12 months means the next AI-found bug may never
    be fixed. Replace those plugins, or cover them with a narrow manifest entry so only the routes you use are
    reachable.</li>
    <li><strong>Use the route manifest as an audit.</strong> In <code>observe</code> mode,
    <a href="/docs/ai-defense/allowlisting#learn">manifest learning</a> lists every public endpoint real customers
    use. A plugin with zero legitimate hits is a removal candidate.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="wordpress">WordPress Configuration</h2>
  <p>Settings that close whole bug classes regardless of which plugin has the bug:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Closes</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>define('DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT', true);</code></td><td>Admin theme/plugin editor as an RCE path after an admin-takeover bug</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>define('DISALLOW_FILE_MODS', true);</code></td><td>Installing a malicious plugin from a hijacked admin session. Do updates through the dashboard's <a href="/docs/engine/wordpress-management">WordPress Update</a> flow instead</td></tr>
    <tr><td>XML-RPC off (or manifest-excluded)</td><td>Credential amplification and pingback abuse; already scored by <code>GENERIC-WP-004</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>REST <code>/wp/v2/users</code> restricted to authenticated users</td><td>User enumeration, the usual first step of an agent's reconnaissance; already scored by <code>GENERIC-WP-003</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>No PHP execution in <code>wp-content/uploads/</code></td><td>The second half of almost every file-upload RCE: the file is written but can't run</td></tr>
    <tr><td>PHP <code>disable_functions</code> for <code>exec</code>, <code>system</code>, <code>passthru</code>, <code>shell_exec</code>, <code>proc_open</code>, <code>popen</code></td><td>Turning a code-execution bug into shell access, including runtime bugs such as <a href="/docs/ai-defense/black-october#old">PHP2Shell</a></td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="containment">Post-Exploit Containment</h2>
  <p>The project's container topology gives containment controls a natural home. Some are shipped, some
  proposed:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Egress deny for the storefront (proposed).</strong> The production WordPress container needs to
    reach its database and, during updates, wordpress.org. It shouldn't open arbitrary outbound connections.
    Putting it on an <code>internal: true</code> Docker network, with an explicit egress proxy only for update
    windows, stops reverse shells, second-stage downloads and data exfiltration over HTTP. That is most of what
    an AI-written post-exploitation script does next.</li>
    <li><strong>Read-only code (proposed).</strong> Mount WordPress core, plugins and themes read-only in
    production, with only <code>uploads/</code> writable (and non-executable). A webshell drop then fails,
    whatever bug delivered it.</li>
    <li><strong>Dropped privileges (partly shipped).</strong> Services in the database-proxy layer already run with
    <code>no-new-privileges</code> and <code>cap_drop</code>. Extending the same profile to every storefront
    container limits container-escape follow-ups.</li>
    <li><strong>Separate database credentials (shipped).</strong> Every pool has its own database user and password
    (see <a href="/docs/operations/hardening">Production Hardening</a>), so credentials stolen from a decoy are
    worthless against production. Applying least privilege to the production DB user (no <code>FILE</code>, no
    <code>SUPER</code>) blocks SQL-injection-to-file-write chains.</li>
    <li><strong>Two-host split (shipped).</strong> In the <a href="/docs/operations/two-host">two-host
    topology</a> the SIEM accepts telemetry over an outbound-only mTLS channel, so a compromised edge can't
    rewrite the evidence of its own compromise.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="updates">Updates Without the Gamble</h2>
  <p>
    Fast patching still matters. It closes the window an attacker's agent is racing through. The usual reason
    shops delay is fear of breaking checkout. The project's
    <a href="/docs/engine/wordpress-management">WordPress Update</a> page already upgrades core and WooCommerce
    across production and every pool behind a maintenance page. Two proposed extensions turn that into a fast,
    low-risk patch path:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Pool-first canary.</strong> Apply the update to one decoy pool first and run the dashboard's
    existing scenario tests (browse, cart, checkout) against it. Promote to production only if they pass.</li>
    <li><strong>Plugin updates through the same flow.</strong> Extend the updater from core and WooCommerce to
    individual plugins, so an AI-era advisory for one plugin can be rolled out in minutes with a tested
    rollback (the engine's <a href="/docs/engine#backups">backups</a>), not deferred for weeks.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The same applies to the runtime under WordPress. Images pin a PHP branch (<code>-php8.4</code>, previously
  <code>-php8.1</code>) and Kibana bundles its own Node, and either can fall behind a fix while the tag still
  looks current. The
  <a href="/docs/ai-defense/black-october">Black October case study</a> shows what that costs.</p>
  <p>Hardening and allowlisting decide <em>whether</em> a shop survives the gap between disclosure and
  patch. Pool-first updates shrink the gap.</p>

  <h2 id="checklist">Checklist</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Step</th><th>Effort</th><th>Status in project</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Admin origin allowlist (<code>ip_whitelist</code> + VPN)</td><td>Low</td><td>Supported today</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Delete inactive plugins and themes</td><td>Low</td><td>Operator action</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT</code> / <code>DISALLOW_FILE_MODS</code></td><td>Low</td><td>Operator action</td></tr>
    <tr><td>No PHP in uploads, <code>disable_functions</code></td><td>Low</td><td><code>disable_functions</code> shipped in the hardened WordPress image; uploads: operator action</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unique pool DB credentials</td><td>Low</td><td>Shipped, verified by <code>test_pool_isolation.sh</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Route manifest in <code>observe</code> mode</td><td>Medium</td><td>Proposed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Egress-deny storefront network</td><td>Medium</td><td>Proposed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Read-only code mounts</td><td>Medium</td><td>Proposed</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pool-first plugin updates</td><td>Medium</td><td>Proposed (core/WooCommerce shipped)</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="honest">Honest Assessment</h2>
  <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Every control is independent of which vulnerability is found next, and most are
  configuration rather than code. Containment turns "AI found an RCE" into "AI found a sandbox with no network".</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> Egress deny, read-only mounts and plugin-level pool-first updates are not yet
  shipped. Some plugins legitimately need outbound access (payment gateways, shipping APIs), which must be
  allowlisted per destination. Hardening reduces impact but doesn't stop a data-reading bug (for example SQL
  injection that only reads) from reading what the DB user can see.</p>
</DocsLayout>
